===South Africa vs Mexico===
South Africa vs Mexico was the [[List of FIFA World Cup opening matches|opening match]] of the World Cup, held on 11 June 2010.<ref name="Mirror">{{cite news|url=http://www.mirrorfootball.co.uk/news/South-Africa-1-1-Mexico-The-Daily-Mirror-match-report-Tragedy-marks-vibrant-World-Cup-opening-match-but-the-hosts-will-continue-to-party-article460181.html|title=South Africa 1-1 Mexico: The Daily Mirror match report|last=Holt|first=Oliver|date=11 June 2010|work=[[Daily Mirror]]|publisher=[[Trinity Mirror]]|access-date=29 June 2010| archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20100612220340/http://www.mirrorfootball.co.uk/news/South-Africa-1-1-Mexico-The-Daily-Mirror-match-report-Tragedy-marks-vibrant-World-Cup-opening-match-but-the-hosts-will-continue-to-party-article460181.html| archive-date= 12 June 2010 | url-status= live}}</ref> It was described as an "enthralling" and "pulsating" match. Mexico put the ball in the net first, but it was offside. South Africa opened the scoring in the 55th minute after [[Siphiwe Tshabalala]] scored with a left foot shot to the top right corner of the net from the left of the penalty area after a pass through Mexico's defence by [[Kagiso Dikgacoi]]. Mexico's captain [[Rafael Márquez]] equalised following a [[corner kick]] in the 79th minute with a shot to the right of the net from five yards out on the right.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.abc.net.au/news/stories/2010/06/12/2925356.htm |title=South Africa draws in pulsating WC opener |last=Maasdorp |first=James |date=12 June 2010 |work=[[ABC News (Australia)|ABC News]] |publisher=[[Australian Broadcasting Corporation]] |access-date=29 June 2010 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100614193157/http://www.abc.net.au/news/stories/2010/06/12/2925356.htm |archive-date=14 June 2010 |url-status=dead }}</ref> In the final minutes of the match, [[Katlego Mphela]] almost scored a winning goal for South Africa, but his shot bounced off the post.<ref name="Mirror"/>

Tshabalala was named as the [[man of the match]]. South Africa's coach, [[Carlos Alberto Parreira]] called the result "fair", while Mexico's coach [[Javier Aguirre]] stated "we could have won, we could have lost".<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.independent.co.uk/sport/football/world-cup/marquez-damps-down-incendiary-show-of-south-african-pride-1998176.html |archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/archive/20220501/https://www.independent.co.uk/sport/football/world-cup/marquez-damps-down-incendiary-show-of-south-african-pride-1998176.html |archive-date=1 May 2022 |url-access=subscription|title=Marquez damps down incendiary show of South African pride|last=Tongue|first=Steve|date=12 June 2010|work=[[The Independent]]|publisher=Independent Print Limited|access-date=29 June 2010}}{{cbignore}}</ref>

This opening match was played again as the opener for the [[2026 FIFA World Cup]] on 11 June 2026, exactly 16 years later, with Mexico now hosting South Africa.

